\section{Model Selection}
\subsection{General Observations}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Medium tweets (20 total):}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Phi4 almost always selects the neutral option.
        \item Llama sometimes selects neutral options.
        \item GPT-OSS almost never selects neutral.
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item \textbf{Rich tweets (88 total):}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item All models produced the same belief inference, but speculation scores and interpretations differ.
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item \textbf{Model tendencies:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Phi4 captures many details but can get lost in them.
        \item Llama is concise but sometimes omits or misinterprets important points.
        \item GPT-OSS is the most consistent and stable overall.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Model Analysis}

\subsubsection{Phi4}
\textbf{Strengths}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Reasoning chains are thorough and reflect available information.
    \item Generally follows guidelines when answering.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Weaknesses}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Does not strictly follow the required answer format.
    \item Long reasoning chains may waste tokens.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{By tweet type}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sparse:} Results may be incomplete, but inference is logical.
    \item \textbf{Medium:} Almost always selects neutral.
    \item \textbf{Rich:} Correctly identifies political leaning, but reasoning may contain inaccuracies (e.g., confusion between gender and race).
    \item \textbf{Very rich:} Struggles with large inputs; token limits can truncate outputs.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Llama}
\textbf{Strengths}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Clear analysis and concise reasoning.
    \item Effectively summarizes key observations.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Weaknesses}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Does not strictly follow the required format.
    \item May ignore or misinterpret key evidence (e.g., sarcasm).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{By tweet type}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sparse:} May produce arbitrary results instead of neutral.
    \item \textbf{Medium:} Less likely than Phi4 to choose neutral.
    \item \textbf{Rich:} Accurately identifies political leaning with reasonable speculation scores.
    \item \textbf{Very rich:} Occasionally misinterprets attributes (e.g., age).
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{GPT-OSS}
\textbf{Strengths}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Strictly follows the required answer format.
    \item Sensitive to subtle political cues (e.g., detecting coded language missed by others).
    \item Rarely selects neutral, except when no relevant information exists.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Weaknesses}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Tends to assign lower speculation scores overall.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example: Speculation Score Differences}

\textbf{Race-related issues}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Phi4: Medium-high speculation (based on inferred political stance).
    \item Llama: Medium-low speculation (political leaning seen as only suggestive).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Israel/Hamas issue}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Phi4: Medium-low speculation (one related tweet present).
    \item Llama: Medium-high speculation (argues the tweet is not directly about the conflict).\textit{Example tweet:} ``HERO: President of Pomona College Suspends Radical Anti-Israel Students and Has Them Arrested (VIDEO)''
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Issue with gpt-oss -- internal inconsistency}
Stance: White people benefit a great deal from societal advantages not available to black people\\
Explanation: The user’s profile explicitly states “Strong Christian morals and values, with conservative Republican worth.” Their tweet history is filled with right‑leaning, pro‑Trump, anti‑left content from outlets such as Breitbart, Gateway Pundit, and OANN. While the tweets never directly address race‑based privilege, the pattern of aligning with conservative media that often downplays systemic racism strongly suggests the user would endorse the view that white people receive substantial societal advantages. This inference is indirect but consistent with the ideological cues present.\\
Unless the model picked up that somehow the user is like a white supremacist and that they're happy about white poeple being advantaged. it doesn't make that much sense because the republicna position is that they're not, that everyone's on the same level playing field so to speak

